<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael of Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1596">1596</a>–<a href="/years/1645">1645</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Michael Romanov, Michael I of Russia, Mikhail Fyodorovich Romanov · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">House of Romanov</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> (Mikhail Fyodorovich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov</a>) was the first tsar of the Romanov dynasty, elected by an assembly of the land in 1613 to end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a>; the news reached him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipatiev-monastery">Ipatiev Monastery</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostroma">Kostroma</a>. His reign, guided at first by his father Patriarch Filaret, restored order and made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and his dynasty ruled Russia until 1917.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-of-russia.jpg" alt="Michael of Russia" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Heinrich Wedekind · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michael-I-Romanov-Wedekind_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Michael, in Russian Mikhail Fyodorovich Romanov, was born on 22 July 1596 (O.S. 12 July), the son of Feodor Nikitich Romanov, later Patriarch Filaret, and Xenia Shestova. He was a first cousin once removed of Feodor I, the last tsar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a>. In 1600 Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a> falsely accused his father of treason, and Michael and his mother were exiled to Beloozero.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> were expelled during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/time-of-troubles">Time of Troubles</a>, a patriotic mood swept the Russian elite. Michael was chosen partly because of his father's martyr-like captivity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and partly because his youth made him seem easily manipulated. On 21 February 1613 some 700 delegates of the Zemsky Sobor agreed on him as a compromise candidate. They did not find him and his mother at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipatiev-monastery">Ipatiev Monastery</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostroma">Kostroma</a> until 24 March, and he was crowned on 21 July 1613, his seventeenth birthday.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stolbovo">Treaty of Stolbovo</a> of 17 February 1617 ended the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>, and Sweden recognised Michael as its ruler. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-deulino">Truce of Deulino</a> of 1 December 1618 ended the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and cost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, but brought back Filaret, who became Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and the effective ruler of Russia until his death in 1633. In the Treaty of Polyanovka in 1634 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-iv-vasa">Władysław IV Vasa</a> gave up his claim to the Russian throne. Meanwhile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, financed by the Stroganov merchant family, continued the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, and Russian explorers reached the Pacific before the reign ended.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Michael married three times. His first betrothed, Maria Khlopova, was deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> after falling ill, and his second wife died four months after the wedding in 1625. His third wife, Eudoxia Streshneva, whom he married in 1626, bore ten children, four of whom reached adulthood, including his successor Alexis. A horse accident early in his life left him with a leg injury that kept him from walking toward the end. He fell ill in April 1645 and died on 23 July 1645. He was a gentle and pious prince who effaced himself behind his counsellors, and the first tsar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">House of Romanov</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michael of Russia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Assemblies of the land (zemskie sobory) met from 1549 and were at their most important in the seventeenth century, when one elected <b>Michael Romanov</b> tsar in 1613 and another approved the law code of 1649, the Sobornoe Ulozhenie, which remained the basis of Russian law until the 1830s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ipatiev Monastery near the town later played a part in the election of <b>Michael Romanov</b> in 1613.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kostroma">Principality of Kostroma</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1627" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Michael of Russia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1627">What was here in 1627? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
